# two references written as the class or null, and three names that the class does not list: an interface that it implements writes them, optional, as the class or null, and a function stores them through that interface.
# with boxed slots the two and the first late name fill a cell of 48, and the other two late names lie out of line, where a read goes through a stub. Narrow forms leave the cell at 48 bytes, so it does not get smaller:
# but a layout with a name out of line that could move in gains from them all the same: the three narrow slots end at 36, and the twelve bytes that are left take the other two late names, four bytes each. Nothing lies out of line any more.
# -- source: narrow-forms-make-room-for-late-names-where-the-cell-stays-as-large.ts
# declare function sink(...values: unknown[]): void;
